<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · song</p><h1 class="enc-title">The Wild Colonial Boy</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">19th century; sung at Glenrowan in June <a href="/years/1880">1880</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">&quot;The Wild Colonial Boy&quot; (Roud 677, Laws L20) is a traditional Irish-Australian folk ballad by an unknown author. It tells of a bushranger in early colonial <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a> who dies in a gunfight with the local police, and it romanticises his fight against colonial authority. It is sung to an earlier Scottish melody, &quot;The Black Douglas&quot;.</p></div>
